nd: no `.dcd/.xtc/.nc/.pdb/.gro/.h5`, no force-field or system-definition files, no `datasets` entry in the task spec (`\"datasets\": []`).\n- Software environment: Python 3.14.4 with **no** scientific stack \u2014 numpy, scipy, pandas, torch, mdtraj, MDAnalysis, ase, openmm, scikit-learn, matplotlib, h5py, netCDF4, pytest all `ModuleNotFoundError` (only PyYAML importable among what I probed). `git/gcc/g++/make/pip3/tar/unzip` exist, but **network is disabled**, so nothing can be installed here. No GPU (`gpu_enabled: false`), 224 CPU cores, ~2 TB RAM, 47 GB free on the workspace mount, ~1 TB tmpfs at `/tmp`.\n\nConsequence: everything below is a **design**, and any real run must declare a different, provisioned environment (torch + MDAnalysis/mdtraj + numpy/scipy, GPU) plus a source for full-rate reference trajectories. No experiment was run and I am not claiming one was.\n\n## 2) Approach sketch (structure only; parameters deliberately deferred)\n\n**Framing decision that shapes the whole design.** Subsampling doesn't merely *blur* fast motion, it *aliases* it: a torsional libration with period shorter than 2\u00b7\u0394t_train reappears as a spurious low-frequency component. So \"recovery\" cannot mean reproducing the reference trajectory frame-by-frame (fast-mode **phase** at t\u2080 is not identifiable from sparse frames). I therefore define the target as **statistical recovery of the fast band**: mode frequencies, band-limited power (equipartition), dephasing/decay times, and amplitude distributions \u2014 with per-trajectory phase reported only via circular statistics. This is the single most consequential choice and I flag it for your approval; it changes every metric below.\n\n**Piece A \u2014 ground-truth full-rate trajectory and fast probes** *(to be specified later)*\n- A reference trajectory saved at a frame interval whose Nyquist frequency sits comfortably above the highest torsional libration of interest (I expect this to force \u0394t_full in the ~1\u20132 fs range for bond-angle-scale librations and ~5\u201310 fs for torsional barriers; the actual number will be set by measuring the reference PSD first, not assumed).\n- Candidate systems, cheapest-first, to be chosen once compute is fixed: (i) a small explicitly-torsioned testbed (dialanine/n-butane-like rotor) where ground truth is cheap to generate or semi-analytic, (ii) a vacuum/explicit-solvent peptide in the style of the original MDGEN torsion-angle setting, (iii) a provided published trajectory if one exists. Since no data ships with the task, an **explicit-solvent peptide plus a cheap rotor testbed** is my default pairing (the testbed gives known frequencies and a \"physics-true\" null; the peptide gives biological relevance).\n- Fast observables: backbone \u03c6/\u03c8 and side-chain \u03c7 dihedrals **restricted to within-basin segments** (large-amplitude barrier crossings are slow dynamics and would contaminate the fast band), plus a deliberately chosen set of high-frequency librational modes identified from the reference PSD. Analysis on *unwrapped* angles (plus cos/sin for bounded quantities) so periodicity doesn't distort spectra.\n\n**Piece B \u2014 subsampled training regime and the comparison** *(to be specified later)*\n- Derive training sets from the *same* reference trajectory by taking every k-th frame, sweeping k across two structurally different regimes: an **aliasing regime** (fast mode period < 2\u00b7k\u00b7\u0394t_full, so the mode is sub-Nyquist) and a **supercritical regime** (fast oscillation entirely unresolved, i.e. the practical MDGEN setting where the training interval is orders of magnitude longer than a libration period).\n- Train the same architecture, parameter budget, optimizer schedule and epoch/step budget at every k, with fixed seeds and matched effective data volume.\n- Evaluation protocol: generate from a set of **held-out reference initial frames** (never in training), equal-length rollouts per model, and compare against held-out reference segments from the same initial conditions. Explicit train/test split by trajectory segment to prevent memorization artifacts.\n\n**Piece C \u2014 analysis quantifying fast-dynamics recovery** *(metric definitions deferred)*\n- *Spectral:* Welch/periodogram PSD per mode on unwrapped angles; peak-frequency error, peak width (= dephasing rate), integrated fast-band power with an **equipartition check** (each quadratic-ish mode should carry ~kT/2; too little power = lost dynamics, too much = injected noise), and a low/high-band power ratio. Subsampled-derived surrogates should show a flat/aliased fast band, the reference a structured one \u2014 the gap is the measurable.\n- *Time domain:* band-pass-filtered oscillation envelope fidelity, instantaneous-frequency and inter-zero-crossing-interval distributions, wavelet time-frequency energy in the fast band.\n- *Memory:* normalized torsional autocorrelation at short lags with fitted decay times; short-time ACF decay is the sharpest discriminator of \"fast dynamics present but mis-phased\" vs \"fast dynamics absent\".\n- *Per-mode amplitude/phase:* multi-sinusoid (or EMD/SSA) decomposition; compare amplitude spectra quantitatively, and phase only as a distribution (Rayleigh/uniformity circular tests) \u2014 with the explicit note that coherent phase is *not* expected and its absence is not failure under my framing.\n- *Slow structural sanity baselines:* RMSD, Rg, RDF/SDF, \u03c6/\u03c8 free-energy surfaces, H-bond occupancies, and metastable-transition rates from a coarse MSM. These guard against the trivial way to \"recover fast dynamics\" \u2014 corrupting the distribution.\n- *Decision statistic:* a pre-registered **recovery ratio** \u03c1 = (S_model \u2212 S_null)/(S_ref \u2212 S_null) in the fast band (S = spectral/ACF discrepancy), with block-bootstrap CIs over segments and across seeds; pre-registered pass band (e.g. \u03c1 \u2265 0.5 with CI excluding 0.25) plus pass/fail on each slow-structural sanity check. Numbers to be finalized with you.\n\n**Piece D \u2014 controls and negative controls**\n1. *Null / trivial baselines:* phase-scrambled phase-preserving surrogates; **cubic-spline interpolation** between subsampled frames (expected: smooth, zero fast power \u2014 the canonical failure); **AR/VAR or OU-fitted-to-subsampled-data** (expected: reddened continuum, no sharp mode peaks).\n2. *Naive-upsampling control:* interpolation + injected white noise tuned to match reference fast-band power. Essential: it produces \"fast power\" with correct amplitude but no mode structure, so it demonstrates that **power \u2260 recovery** and forces the PSD-shape/ACF metrics to do the work.\n3. *Positive controls:* model trained on **full-rate** data (upper bound \u2014 proves the metric can register recovery when it exists) and an oracle conditioned on true intervening frames.\n4. *Ablation of the fast-dynamics component:* strip the autoregressive multi-frame time-translation conditioning (single-frame CVAE \u2192 frame-wise sampling with no intra-interval trajectory), replace the stochastic latent prior with posterior-mean sampling (deterministic \u2192 should collapse oscillations to fixed points), and shorten the conditioning window N. Expect selective loss of the fast band while marginal torsion distributions survive \u2014 the specificity test.\n5. *Longer-subsample-interval condition:* the k-sweep above, to locate the crossover where \u03c1 collapses toward 0 (information-theoretic floor) and to test whether the *alias* frequency is wrongly reproduced.\n6. *Anti-hallucination negative control:* a deliberately low-pass-filtered / mode-suppressed \"reference\" with no fast dynamics; a correct model should **not** invent fast-band power there. This is what makes a positive result meaningful rather than an artifact of noise injection.\n7. *Transfer/memorization controls:* train-on-A-test-on-B; and a tiny-subset overfit check.\n\n**Feasible checks in *this* environment:** only design-level ones \u2014 schema/config authoring, a closed-form toy-oscillator calculation of expected aliasing and Nyquist limits for candidate k values (needs numpy, currently absent), and static review of metric definitions. Actual training/inference requires a provisioned environment; I would not attempt it here given no scientific Python stack and no network.\n\n## 3) Consequential assumptions (source-supported vs. my own design choices)\n\nSource-supported: design-only task, repos/compute explicitly not required, \"do not claim an experiment was run,\" no bundled datasets, network disabled, no GPU, no scientific Python present.\n\nMy own design choices / external priors, not verified against any source here:\n- **MDGEN architecture and usage** (conditional VAE over torsions with an autoregressive time-translation module conditioning on N past subsampled frames) is my external recollection of the method, **not** anything readable in /workspace. If the intended codebase differs, Pieces B/D4 change materially.\n- **Recovery = statistical, not per-trajectory phase** (the framing decision above). If you intend trajectory-level phase fidelity, the design becomes an identifiability argument and would likely answer \"no\" by construction.\n- **Fast-timescale targets** are assumed to be within-basin torsional librations / ps-scale angle oscillations; the actual band must be measured from the reference PSD before choosing \u0394t_full and k.\n- **Subsampling factors**: I assume a k-sweep spanning mild-aliasing to ~10\u00b3\u00d7 is the informative range, with the practical MDGEN regime near the top; the exact values depend on the measured fast band.\n- **Data/compute**: I assume either the ability to generate or obtain full-rate reference trajectories and that MDGEN-sized training is single-GPU, hours-per-config scale. If neither is available, the fallback is the toy-rotor/anharmonic-oscillator testbed as primary (weaker biological claim). No runtime limit was given to me; I am assuming a per-config budget of hours, not days, and that a few tens of training runs (k-sweep \u00d7 seeds \u00d7 ablations) is acceptable.\n- Velocities/short-lag ACF may be unavailable if only coordinates are stored, in which case all fast-band inference is coordinate-only.\n\nQuestions I'd like answered before the full design (4 most load-bearing): (1) is there a specific MDGEN repo/checkpoint and dataset we must target? (2) may reference MD be generated, or must a provided dataset be used? (3) is a GPU and a provisioned torch/MDAnalysis environment available for the eventual run? (4) do you accept the statistical-recovery framing, and roughly how many training runs / what wall-clock is in budget?", "evidence": "Repository/environment observations (all from exploratory commands; no experiment run):\n1. `/workspace` listing: only `task.json` (464 B). `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` returns just those two entries. No source, configs, manifests, README, or track.md file.\n2. `find / -iname \"*mdgen*\" -not -path \"/proc/*\"` \u2192 empty output. No MDGEN code anywhere on the filesystem. `/home/actor`, `/tmp`, `/opt` are empty; no `~/.cache/pip`.\n3. `/workspace/task.json` contents confirm: question + context (\"Do not claim that an experiment was run\"), `\"datasets\": []`, and `\"documents\": {\"track.md\": \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"}` \u2014 the only 'track.md' that exists.\n4. Python 3.14.4. Import probe: numpy, scipy, pandas, torch, mdtraj, ase, openmm, MDAnalysis, sklearn, matplotlib, netCDF4, h5py, pytest all ModuleNotFoundError; only PyYAML 6.0.3 imports. Network disabled \u21d2 cannot install.\n5. Binaries: git, gcc, g++, make, python3, pip3, tar, unzip present; no nvcc, no gmx, no mpirun. `gpu_enabled: false`, 224 cores (`nproc`), ~2011 GB RAM, 47 GB free on the /workspace NFS mount, 1 TB tmpfs at /tmp.\n6. No molecular-data files (dcd/xtc/nc/pdb/gro/h5) supplied with the task.\nImplication: the task is design-only as stated, and any executable run needs an externally provisioned environment and data source.", "assumptions": ["FIXED BY TASK: design-only; no experiment may be claimed as run; repositories/compute are not required.", "FIXED BY TASK: no datasets are bundled (datasets: []) and no MDGEN source exists in the workspace.", "FIXED BY ENVIRONMENT: network disabled, no scientific Python (numpy/scipy/torch/mdtraj) installed, no GPU.", "DESIGN CHOICE: 'recovery' is defined as statistical recovery of the fast band (frequencies, band power/equipartition, ACF decay, amplitude distributions), not frame-by-frame phase fidelity.", "DESIGN CHOICE (external knowledge, unverified here): MDGEN = conditional VAE over torsion angles with an autoregressive time-translation module conditioning on N past subsampled frames.", "DESIGN CHOICE: fast-timescale targets are within-basin torsional librations at ps-scale; the actual band will be measured from the reference PSD rather than assumed.", "DESIGN CHOICE: a k-sweep spanning mild-aliasing to ~10^3x subsampling is the informative range for the training regime.", "DESIGN CHOICE: reference/full-rate frames saved at ~1-10 fs so the fast band is Nyquist-resolved (value to be set after measuring the reference spectrum).", "DESIGN CHOICE: pairing a cheap anharmonic-rotor testbed (known frequencies, cheap ground truth) with a peptide system for biological relevance.", "ASSUMED RESOURCE (not stated by task): MDGEN-sized training is single-GPU, hours-per-config, and a few tens of runs (k-sweep x seeds x ablations) is within budget.", "ASSUMED: either MD can be generated or a published full-rate trajectory can be obtained; if neither, the toy testbed becomes the primary system."], "uncertainties": ["Which MDGEN codebase/version, checkpoint, and dataset the final experiment must target \u2014 none is present in the workspace; my architecture description is external recollection and may be wrong.", "Whether the intended notion of 'accurately recover' is statistical or trajectory-level (with phase); the latter is likely unanswerable positively from aliased training data.", "Actual frequency range of the fast torsional modes for the chosen system, which determines both Delta_t_full and the admissible subsampling factors.", "No stated wall-clock or compute ceiling; my hours-per-config and ~dozens-of-runs budget is my own guess.", "Whether reference trajectories ship velocities (needed for the sharpest short-lag ACF diagnostic) or coordinates only.", "Whether explicit-solvent peptide MD is in scope at all, or whether only a provided dataset may be used \u2014 this dominates total cost.", "Statistical power: how many independent seeds/segments are enough to resolve the recovery ratio; needs a small calibration before fixing the pre-registered threshold.", "No environment here can execute even the analysis code (no numpy/scipy), so all numerical thresholds remain unvalidated by feasible check."], "summary": "Orientation of empty workspace plus approach-level plan for MDGEN fast-dynamics recovery experiment"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-e8b92711-0913-46e8-acaa-d1fcde5e0374", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Orientation of empty workspace plus approach-level plan for MDGEN fast-dynamics recovery experiment", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
