{"id": "a7f55021-0092-4000-84ec-f4fcef878f4e", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T10:50:07.312587", "source": "user", "parent_id": "224c10fe-9223-43aa-b7a4-51463796b86e", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{"researcher_request": "Approach approved. The framing is sound and I want you to proceed to the full design. Answers to your four load-bearing questions, staying strictly within what the project supplies:\n\n1) Target repo/checkpoint/dataset: none exists. Per your inspection the workspace holds only task.json, no MDGEN source, no datasets entry, no trajectories. So the design must be written as method-agnostic-but-concretely-instantiated: state the assumed MDGEN interface (conditional generative model over torsion angles with an autoregressive time-translation conditioning module taking N prior subsampled frames) as an explicit, labeled assumption, and specify the interface contract the experiment actually depends on (input: N past frames at interval k*dt; output: a length-k intra-interval trajectory; stochastic latent prior). Where a design element would change if the real codebase differs, flag it inline rather than silently betting on your recollection.\n\n2) Reference data: nothing is provided, so the design may specify generating reference MD, but it must include a data-provenance branch (generate vs. obtain a published full-rate trajectory vs. toy-rotor-only fallback) with the claims each branch can and cannot support. Keep the toy/anharmonic-rotor testbed as the load-bearing component, not a throwaway, since it is the only arm with semi-analytic ground truth and it is the arm that survives the no-compute fallback.\n\n3) Compute: the project explicitly says repositories and compute are not required, and no ceiling or availability is stated. Do not assume a budget; instead give a tiered feasibility plan (minimal / standard / full) with the run counts each tier needs and which conclusions each tier can actually support, and mark the minimal tier as the recommended default.\n\n4) Framing: I accept statistical recovery of the fast band as the primary target, with two conditions. (a) The design must contain an explicit identifiability section stating when trajectory-level phase fidelity is recoverable in principle from k-subsampled data versus information-theoretically lost, and must state that under the strongly-subsampled regime a phase-fidelity reading of \"accurately recover\" answers \"no\" by construction rather than by model failure. Treat that as a finding the design is meant to expose, not a bug to design around. (b) The headline question (\"does MDGEN recover the fast dynamics missing from subsampled trajectories?\") must map onto a primary endpoint that could in principle return a negative; do not let the recovery-ratio threshold and pass band be tunable after the fact, so state them as pre-registered proposals with the rationale for each number, marked as proposals for you and me to fix before any run.\n\nAdditional requirements for the full design:\n- Define the k-sweep in terms of the ratio between the fast-band period and the training interval (sub-Nyquist / near-Nyquist / strongly-supercritical), with absolute k values derived from the measured reference PSD rather than asserted up front. Your 1-10 fs dt_full and ~10^3x upper end are acceptable as illustrative, labeled values only.\n- Keep these four arms mandatory, since the design's credibility rests on them: the naive-upsampling control (matched fast-band power, no mode structure) that forces PSD-shape and ACF metrics rather than raw power to do the work; the full-rate positive control that proves the metric can register recovery; the anti-hallucination negative control (mode-suppressed reference) that prevents noise injection from masquerading as recovery; and the ablation of the time-translation/stochastic-latent conditioning that tests specificity rather than global degradation. State the expected signature of each so a reviewer can falsify the design.\n- Include the within-basin-segmentation rule for torsions as a first-class analysis decision, with how segments are defined and a sensitivity check showing the fast-band conclusion is not an artifact of that segmentation, since large-amplitude barrier crossings contaminating the fast band is the most likely way this design silently fails.\n- The slow structural sanity baselines must be presented as veto conditions on a positive fast-band result, not as co-equal success metrics.\n- Handle the coordinates-only uncertainty explicitly: give the metric set under velocities-available and coordinates-only, and say which diagnostics are lost in the latter.\n\nDeliverable is the design itself: rationale, controls, analysis plan with concrete estimators and the recovery-ratio decision statistic, feasibility tiers, and a limitations section covering identifiability and the assumption-dependence on the MDGEN interface. No installs, no training, no inference, and no fabricated numbers; where a value is needed that cannot be obtained here, present it as a formula plus a labeled illustrative value, and you may include the closed-form Nyquist/aliasing derivation symbolically. Do not claim any experiment was run.", "required_handoff_stage": "result", "approach_approved": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "6a79855a-2dc0-4b3c-9b14-44f5c6803aa1", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T10:52:54.401587", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "a7f55021-0092-4000-84ec-f4fcef878f4e", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "I need to produce the full design. Let me do a couple of quick feasibility checks that are legitimate here: symbolic Nyquist/aliasing derivation could be done with pure Python (no numpy). Maybe I can verify aliasing math with pure python math module — e.g., demonstrate that a sinusoid at frequency f sampled at interval τ appears at aliased frequency |f - round(f/τ⁻¹)/τ|, and compute illustrative values for candidate dt_full and k. That's a legitimate analytic sanity check, not an experiment on the task question. Let me do it to ground the formulas and produce illustrative tables.

Also I can check workspace files once more. Let me run a pure-python aliasing calculation.

Let me define: dt_full = 2 fs (frame output interval). Torsional libration period T_fast = 200 fs (frequency 5 THz ≈ 167 cm⁻¹... let's be careful: 1 ps⁻¹ = 1 THz ≈ 33.356 cm⁻¹. A libration at 100 cm⁻¹ = 3 THz → period 0.33 ps = 333 fs.)

So compute for k values: k=2 (4 fs), k=8 (16 fs), k=32 (64 fs), k=100 (200 fs), k=500 (1 ps), k=2500 (5 ps), k=25000 (50 ps).

Ratio r = T_fast / τ_train, where τ_train = k·dt_full. Nyquist freq of training = 1/(2τ). Mode is resolved if f_mode < 1/(2τ) i.e. τ < T_fast/2, i.e. r > 2. Sub-Nyquist if r < 2.

Also alias frequency formula: f_alias = |f - n·f_s| where f_s = 1/τ, n = round(f·τ).

Also compute the "number of samples per oscillation period" = T_fast/τ = r.

Illustrative: T_fast = 333 fs (100 cm⁻¹ mode), dt_full = 2 fs:
- k=2: τ=4 fs, r=83 → well resolved
- k=32: τ=64, r=5.2 → resolved
- k=83: τ=166, r=2.0 → Nyquist
- k=166: τ=332, r=1.0 → alias to DC (exactly one sample per period) — the mode becomes a static offset.
- k=500: τ=1000 fs, r=0.33 → aliases; f = 3 THz, f_s = 1 THz → alias: n = round(3)=3 → f_alias = |3-3| = 0 → DC again. Interesting.
- k=2500: τ=5 ps, f_s=0.2 THz, f=3 THz → n=15 → alias = 0 again. Ugh, integer multiples alias to DC. Choose non-resonant k values for the sweep — an important design point! If τ_train is an integer multiple of the mode period, the alias falls at zero frequency. Must include the note: choose k values that are NOT near-integer multiples of T_fast/dt_full to avoid DC-aliasing artifacts, and deliberately include some near-resonant ones as a diagnostic test.

Actually a better design: derive k from measured PSD, defining regimes by r = T_mode/τ:
- Resolved control arm: r ≥ 8 (≥8 samples/period)
- Near-Nyquist: r ∈ [2, 4]
- Sub-Nyquist mild aliasing: r ∈ [1, 2)
- Strongly supercritical: r ≤ 0.2 (τ ≥ 5 periods) — the practical MDGEN regime.

And note the alias mapping is not monotone; for the strongly supercritical regime the alias frequency is essentially arbitrary/pseudo-random within [0, f_Nyq], and the fast-band structure is scrambled into the low band — that's the aliasing-contamination point, which also motivates a "spectral whitening" statement: for τ >> T, the sampled sequence of a fast oscillation ≈ random draw from its marginal amplitude distribution → contributes a broadband (white-ish) component, not a peak, at frequencies below Nyq_train.

Also there's a nice closed-form for the equipartition/power argument: for a harmonic mode in a Langevin thermostat, coordinate PSD is Lorentzian; undersampling maps it to a flat band. Let me include a symbolic statement: PSD of undersampled series S_τ(f) = Σ_m S(f + m/τ) (folding), so when f_mode >> 1/(2τ) the mode folds into the low band and adds a quasi-constant pedestal. This is exactly why the naive-upsampling control matters and why "the model learned fast dynamics" needs PSD-shape evidence.

