", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a9cd534dd15232a5", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-a9cd534dd15232a5", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"answer": "# Experiment design: can MDGEN recover fast-timestep dynamics lost to subsampling?\n\n**Status / scope.** Design only. Nothing was installed, trained, or inferred; no numbers below are measured. Workspace state unchanged (`/workspace/task.json` is still the only file). Where a value is needed that cannot be obtained here, it is given as a formula with a value labeled **[ILLUS]**. The pure-Python arithmetic I did run is closed-form aliasing/period bookkeeping and a Langevin conditional-mean formula (Section 17), not an experiment.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Claim structure (what a result means)\n\nThe headline question maps to one **primary endpoint** and a set of confirmatory endpoints, vetoes and identifiability claims:\n\n- **H_fast (primary):** a model trained *only* on k-subsampled torsion data generates fast-band spectral/temporal structure that is statistically closer to the full-rate reference than a matched-power naive-upsampling null.\n- **H_spec:** that structure is produced by the component nominally responsible for it (multi-frame time-translation conditioning + stochastic latent prior), not by generic model capacity or injected noise.\n- **H_ident (finding, not a bug):** in the strongly-supercritical regime, *trajectory-level phase* fidelity is information-theoretically unavailable, so a phase-fidelity reading of \"accurately recover\" answers **\"no\" by construction**; the design measures the crossover at which this happens rather than assuming it.\n- **V_fast (vetoed positive):** any H_fast result is void if slow structural vetoes fail or if the anti-hallucination control shows the model invents fast power where none exists.\n\nThe headline answer is **\"no\" unless the primary endpoint passes, the specificity ablation behaves as predicted, the anti-hallucination control passes, and all vetoes hold.** The endpoint can genuinely return negative (Section 15 enumerates a dominant negative branch).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Assumed MDGEN interface (labeled assumption + interface contract)\n\n**[ASSUMPTION — external recollection, no source in workspace]:** MDGEN is a conditional generative model over torsion angles with an autoregressive *time-translation* module conditioned on the N most recent frames, trained on k-subsampled trajectories. No MDGEN code, checkpoint, or config exists in this project.\n\nThe experiment depends on only this **interface contract**, so a different real codebase can be substituted:\n\n| Contract element | Requirement |\n|---|---|\n| `train(D_k)` | `D_k = {θ_{ik}}`: torsion-angle frames at interval τ = k·Δt_full |\n| `generate(θ_{t}, …, θ_{t−(N−1)}, k, seed)` → `{θ̂_{t+1..t+k}}` | emits a length-k intra-interval trajectory on the **same Δt_full grid** as the reference |\n| Stochastic latent prior | rollout must be a *sample*, so M≥100 rollouts per conditioning frame are available at cost ≈ O(M) forward passes |\n| Loss/compute budget configurable | needed to match parameter/step budget across arms |\n| Ablatable conditioning | N settable, and the latent-noise path replaceable by its posterior mean |\n\n**Inline flags where the design changes if the real codebase differs:**\n- If the model outputs **Cartesian coordinates** rather than torsions → add an explicit torsion-extraction step, and RMSD/RDF vetoes become co-supporting rather than purely defensive; the fast-band estimators are unchanged (they act on extracted dihedrals). *Design impact: preprocessing + one extra veto; conclusions unchanged.*\n- If **N is fixed** and cannot be swept → the N-ladder specificity arm is replaced by the *deterministic-latent* ablation alone, and H_spec is weakened to a single-component claim (state this in the limitations of any result).\n- If **rollouts are not reproducible per conditioning frame** (no seed control) → M-rollout ensemble statistics become intractable; replace the ensemble conditional-mean estimator by pooled time-average statistics, at the cost of losing the \"kick-response\" diagnostic (the single most interpretable fast-dynamics statistic). *This is the most damaging incompatibility; it must be checked before any tier is committed.*\n- If a checkpoint is later supplied but its training k is unknown → treat its k as unknown and estimate it by the alias test (Section 6, alias fingerprint); the design still works, but the \"which regime was it trained in\" question becomes an inference rather than a label.\n- If the codebase is non-deterministic across runs → 3 seeds minimum becomes 5, and the seed variance component (Section 13) must be reported.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Systems and data-provenance branch\n\n**Arm T — anharmonic-rotor testbed (mandatory, load-bearing, survives all fallbacks).**\nUnderdamped Langevin dynamics of one or a few torsional rotors in a periodic potential, `dω = [−V'(θ)/I − γω]dt + σdW`, `V(θ) = −V₀cos(θ)`. Ground truth is **semi-analytic**: conditional mean `E[θ(t)|θ₀] = θ₀ e^{−γt/2}[cos(ω̃t) + (γ/2ω̃)sin(ω̃t)]`, `ω̃=√(ω₀²−γ²/4)`; coordinate PSD is a Lorentzian-squared-type resonance with known width `γ`; ACF decay and mode variance (`kT/2` in the harmonic limit) are known in closed form. γ, ω₀, T, Δt_full are all *chosen by us and verified by an integrator step-halving check*, so this arm has an **answer key the molecular arms do not**. Consequences: (i) it is the calibration arm for every estimator and every threshold; (ii) the identifiability crossover can be checked against theory; (iii) it is the only arm that runs with no MD engine and no GPU.\n**Claim it can support:** \"under a known-spectrum generator, the interface either does or does not regenerate a specified resonance from k-subsampled data.\" **Cannot support:** any claim about biomolecular torsions, friction heterogeneity, or anharmonic basin structure.\n\n**Arm M — molecular torsion testbed.** Backbone φ/ψ plus 1–2 side-chain χ dihedrals of a small peptide; default proposal a capped alanine peptide (explicit-solvent if a generated trajectory is available, else whatever the provenance branch permits). **Can support:** whether the arm-T conclusion transfers to real, coupled, anharmonic, friction-heterogeneous torsions. **Cannot support:** statements about a specific published MDGEN result unless its exact training data are reproduced.\n\n**Data-provenance branch (one of A/B/C selected before Step 0):**\n\n| Branch | Requirement | Claims it supports | Claims it cannot |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. Generate reference MD** | an MD engine + force field + Δt_full small enough for the fast band (Section 5) | strongest: full control of Δt_full, velocities, thermostat, and the ability to *construct* the mode-suppressed reference exactly | none structural; cost is the limit |\n| **B. Obtain published full-rate trajectory** | Δt_full of the public archive must satisfy the curvature rule; often only 1–10 ps frames exist → **likely disqualifying** | replication-flavored external validity for the slow/structural vetoes and near-Nyquist regime | any fast-band claim if Δt_full > T_fast/10 — the mode is already destroyed in the \"ground truth\" |\n| **C. Toy-rotor-only fallback** | none (survives no-MD, no-GPU, no-data) | the full primary/identifiability/veto logic, but only on Arm T | every biomolecular statement; headline answer must be phrased \"in a controlled rotor testbed with the assumed interface\" |\n\nBranch **B is gated by a hard pre-check**: compute the archive's Nyquist frequency `f_Nyq = 1/(2Δt_full)` and reject the source for fast-band purposes if `f_Nyq < 1.25 × f_max,fastband`. This is a *design* rejection, stated in advance, not a post-hoc judgment.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Step 0 — measure the fast band before choosing anything\n\nAll absolute k values, Δt_full, and the mode set are **derived, not asserted**:\n1. Compute the reference PSD and ACF of each candidate torsion (unwrapped angle, mean-removed) and each internal coordinate.\n2. Identify peaks in the **fast band** `B = [f_lo, f_hi]`, declared by the rule `f_lo = ` frequency below which the *within-basin* ACF has already decayed (i.e., B contains only modes that oscillate within a basin visit), `f_hi = ` the highest frequency whose ACF-based curvature estimator is stable under step-halving. Illustrative target (from the closed-form check in §17): a peptide torsion libration band of roughly 20–200 cm⁻¹ (periods 1.7 ps down to 0.17 ps) **[ILLUS]**.\n3. Select the **pre-declared mode set** `M = {m₁…m_p}` = the top peaks by integrated band power, `p` capped at 6, frozen *before* any model comparison.\n4. Fix `Δt_full ≤ min_{m∈M} T_m/10` (curvature/PSD-peak stability rule; stricter than Nyquist by 5×). For a 200 cm⁻¹ mode this is Δt_full ≤ 16.7 fs; the commonly imagined \"save every frame\" Δt_full = 1–2 fs is therefore *more* than sufficient **[ILLUS]**, and the design does not depend on it.\n5. Publish `M`, `B`, `Δt_full`, and the reference noise floor `S_ref` (§9) — all before any generative arm is scored. This is what makes the thresholds non-tunable after the fact.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Within-basin segmentation (first-class analysis decision)\n\n**Why it can silently fail:** barrier crossings contribute broadband low-frequency power; the resulting leakage and baseline elevation in the fast band can either **mask** a genuine fast-band deficit (a bad model looks fine) or, conversely, if the reference is segmented but generated data are not (or vice versa), produce a **spurious** apparent deficit. Segmentation is therefore applied with one rule to *both* reference and generated data.\n\n**Rule (deterministic, leakage-free, applied identically to reference and generated):**\n- Basin membership for torsion `m` is defined by **fixed geometric windows** on the torsion value itself: the 3–6 rotameric wells of its marginal free-energy surface, with boundaries at the FES maxima located once on the reference. Memoryless and deterministic ⇒ no state-dependent selection bias, and it needs no velocities or slow variables, so it transfers to generated rollouts unchanged.\n- A **segment** = maximal consecutive run inside one window with length ≥ `L_min = max(C_seg·T_hi, L_train-interval)`; `C_seg = 20` **[ILLUS/proposal]** so a segment supports a Welch PSD with the required resolution, and the second term prevents generated rollouts from contributing short segments the reference lacks (matched-segment-length bootstrap, §13).\n- A **guard band** of width `δ_g` on each side of a window boundary is discarded (`δ_g` = half-width in which the harmonic-in-basin approximation fails, estimated from the reference FES curvature).\n- Segments containing any **other** declared mode's transition are dropped; the drop fraction is reported and must be ≤ 20% **[proposal]**, otherwise the mode set is re-declared.\n- **Matching check:** reference and generated segment-length distributions are compared (KS statistic) before any spectral comparison; unmatched distributions invalidate the comparison, not the model.\n\n**Sensitivity check (mandatory; a result that fails it is reported as indeterminate, not positive):**\n1. Refit the fast-band endpoint with `C_seg ∈ {10, 20, 40}` and `δ_g ∈ {0, δ_g, 2δ_g}` (3×3 grid, reference-only and generated-only), and additionally with an MSM-derived (PCCA+) basin definition instead of the geometric one.\n2. Report the **variance decomposition**: `Var_segmentation-choice / Var_model-difference`. Decision rule (proposal): if segmentation choice accounts for ≥ 50% of the model-difference signal, the fast-band conclusion is **not** reportable.\n3. **Segmentation-artifact null:** run the full segmentation on a synthetic trajectory with **no** fast mode (pure overdamped multi-basin hopping matched to the reference's transition rates and dwell-time distribution). If any fast-band power or any nonzero `ρ` appears, the pipeline manufactures fast dynamics from segmentation and the design is invalidated before it begins. This is the cheapest check in the whole plan and is placed in the minimal tier.\n\n---\n\n## 6. The k-sweep, defined by a dimensionless ratio\n\n**Definition.** Let `T_ref` be the period of the fastest pre-declared mode `m₁`, and `τ = k·Δt_full` the training interval. Define\n\n> `u = τ / T_ref` = training interval in units of the fastest fast-mode period; samples-per-oscillation = `1/u`.\n\nRegimes (mandatory coverage): **resolved** `u ≤ 0.25`; **near-Nyquist** `0.4 ≤ u ≤ 0.6`; **sub-Nyquist/aliased** `0.7 ≤ u ≤ 1.5`; **strongly supercritical** `u ≥ 4`, with the top point chosen to bracket the practical MDGEN-style regime (`u ≳ 10`, i.e. training interval ≳ tens of librations) **[ILLUS range]**.\n\n**Aliasing (closed form, used both to design the sweep and as a diagnostic).** Sampling at `f_s = 1/τ` folds a mode at `f₀` to\n`f_alias = |f₀ − round(f₀τ)/τ| ∈ [0, 1/(2τ)]`.\nTwo design consequences:\n- **Integer-u degeneracy.** When `u` is an integer the fast mode folds **exactly to DC** and appears in the training data as a static offset — zero usable information about the oscillation. Main-sweep `k` values must therefore avoid near-integer `u` (a ±10% keep-out margin), while **one deliberate near-resonant `u ≈ 1` point is retained as a diagnostic**: a model that still shows a genuine `f₀` peak there is generating from physics/prior, not fitting an alias.\n- **Alias fingerprint test.** At every `k`, the subsampled *reference* shows a peak at `f_alias`, not `f₀`. A generated spectrum peaking at `f_alias` is proof of **fitting the aliased data**, and is scored as a failure of H_fast even though it looks like \"spectral agreement\". The primary metric is therefore evaluated against the *full-rate* reference and separately screened for alias-peak contamination.\n\n**Illustrative instantiation** (dt = 2 fs **[ILLUS]**, `T_ref` = 333.6 fs, the period of a 100 cm⁻¹ libration **[ILLUS]**; `k = round(u·T_ref/Δt)`; alias frequencies computed exactly, §17):\n\n| regime | u | k | τ (fs) | f_Nyq (cm⁻¹) | apparent f of the 100 cm⁻¹ mode (cm⁻¹) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| resolved | 0.125 | 21 | 42 | 397 | 100.0 (true) |\n| resolved | 0.25 | 42 | 84 | 199 | 100.0 (true) |\n| near-Nyquist | 0.5 | 83 | 166 | 100.5 | at Nyquist edge (unstable estimate) |\n| near-Nyquist | 0.6 | 100 | 200 | 83.4 | 66.8 (aliased) |\n| sub-Nyquist | 0.8 | 133 | 266 | 62.7 | 25.4 (aliased) |\n| sub-Nyquist / resonant | 1.0 | 167 | 334 | 49.9 | ~0.1 → folds to DC |\n| supercritical | 2.0 | 334 | 668 | 25.0 | ~0.1 (DC) |\n| supercritical | 30 | 5003 | 10006 | 1.7 | ≈0 (DC) |\n| supercritical | 150 | 25017 | 50034 | 0.3 | ≈0 (DC) |\n\nNote the structural message: for the whole supercritical block the training data contain **no resolvable trace** of the mode's frequency — the fast band is absent, not merely noisy. That is exactly the regime the headline question is about, and it is why the primary endpoint is spectral-shape recovery vs. a matched-power null rather than any form of trajectory agreement. `u` values ≥ 100 correspond to the \"~10³× and beyond\" upper end — kept, but explicitly as illustrative scale, not as asserted design values.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Arms and their expected (falsifiable) signatures\n\n**Training arms.** Reference/full-rate model, main model, and ablations all share the **same architecture size, optimization steps, epochs and seeds list**; the only difference is the training sampling interval `k` (and the ablated component). Generated data are produced on the Δt_full grid with matched conditioning-frame counts.\n\n### 7.1 Mandatory control C1 — naive upsampling with matched fast-band power\n*Definition:* cubic-spline interpolation of the k-subsampled torsion onto the Δt_full grid, **plus** a colored-noise field whose PSD is shaped to reproduce the reference fast-band **integrated power** (matched amplitude), with phases drawn uniform-random, so it has **no** mode structure and **no** correct decay.\n*Purpose:* force PSD *shape*, peak position, width and ACF to do the work; raw power is explicitly not evidence.\n*Expected signature if the design is sound:* integrated fast-band power ratio ≈ 1; **no** peak at `f₀`; peak-frequency error undefined/random; linewidth undefined; short-lag ACF ≈ that of the noise field (much faster than reference); ensemble conditional-mean \"ringing\" absent (monotone decay). `ρ_shape ≈ 0` by construction — C1 **is** the null `S_null`.\n*Falsifier for the design:* if C1 scores `ρ_shape > 0.25`, the metric is power-dominated and must be fixed before any model is run.\n\n### 7.2 Mandatory positive control C2 — full-rate positive control\n*Definition:* the identical model trained at `k=1` (Δt_full grid) and evaluated with the identical pipeline; run at every seed and both system arms.\n*Purpose:* proves the metric **can** register recovery, calibrates the floor `S_ref` and the noise floor, and pins down how much of the residual is estimator/rollout noise rather than model failure.\n*Expected signature:* `ρ_shape ≥ 0.8` **[proposal]**; peak-frequency error within the reference bootstrap CI; linewidth ratio ∈ [0.85, 1.15]; short-lag ACF indistinguishable; ensemble conditional mean reproduces damped ringing with `γ̂_gen/γ̂_ref` within CI.\n*Falsifier:* if C2 fails, **every** negative result on the main arm is uninterpretable (pipeline-limited, not model-limited) and must be reported as indeterminate.\n\n### 7.3 Mandatory negative control C3 — anti-hallucination (mode-suppressed reference)\n*Definition:* build a reference in which the declared fast modes are **removed but the slow structure and marginal distributions are preserved**. Two constructions, run as a pair: (i) **post-hoc spectral suppression** — low-pass/IIR notch filtering of the reference at `B`, verified to leave basin statistics unchanged; (ii) **dynamical suppression** (Arm T and, if feasible, Arm M) — increase friction γ until the rotor is critically/over-damped (`ω₀ < γ/2`), eliminating libration while keeping the same barrier crossing statistics.\n*Purpose:* a model that injects generic fast noise passes the naive-upsampling comparison and looks like it \"recovered\" dynamics. This control makes that impossible.\n*Expected signature for a *correct* model:* generated fast-band power in B is **suppressed**, matching the suppressed reference (`ρ_shape` high w.r.t. the suppressed reference and the interpolated null in B, band-power ratio ≈ 1 with the *suppressed* reference, no spurious peak in B).\n*Falsifier:* significant generated power in B against the suppressed reference (bootstrap CI excluding ratio 1 from below) ⇒ the model hallucinates fast-band power ⇒ **all** positive fast-band results are void, and the honest answer becomes \"no; apparent recovery is stochastic noise injection.\"\n\n### 7.4 Mandatory ablation A-spec — specificity of the fast-dynamics component\n*Definition (three variants, all else fixed):* (i) **no time-translation conditioning** — N = 1 conditioning frame; (ii) **deterministic latent** — replace the stochastic prior by posterior-mean sampling (kills intra-interval stochastic dynamics); (iii) **conditioning-window ladder** — N ∈ {1, N/2, N, 2N}.\n*Purpose:* test specificity rather than global degradation.\n*Expected signature (the discriminating pattern):* fast-band metrics degrade **selectively** — `ρ_shape` and short-lag ACF collapse toward the C1 null and ringing vanishes — while the **marginal torsion distributions and slow vetoes remain intact** (RMSD/RDF/FES unchanged within CI). The N-ladder should show a monotone trend.\n*Falsifier / non-informative outcome:* the ablation degrades **everything equally** (slow vetoes also collapse). Then it demonstrates only that the component matters, not that it supplies fast dynamics, and H_spec is **unresolved** — this branch must be reported as such, not as support.\n\n### 7.5 Additional controls (standard/full tiers)\n- **C4 spline-only** (no noise): expected smooth path, fast-band power ≈ 0, `ρ_shape ≈ 0`, quantifies how much of the signal is \"any high-frequency content at all\".\n- **C5 AR(p)/OU/VAR fitted on the subsampled data** (order chosen by AIC on the subsampled series): expected red continuum with a **peak at `f_alias`, not `f₀`** — the sharpest demonstration that fitting subsampled data reproduces the alias.\n- **C6 IAAFT phase-scrambled surrogates** of the reference: null distribution for the PSD/ACF discrepancy statistics (used for CI calibration, not as a model).\n- **C7 rollout-length disentanglement** *(added after checking the interface risk in §2)*: compare the fast band of **single-step** conditional generation against the full k-frame **rollout**. If fast dynamics are present at one step and lost in rollouts, the failure is error accumulation, a mechanistically different (and separately reportable) answer.\n- **C8 learnability/supervised ceiling:** a supervised regressor trained *with access to the true intervening frames* predicts intra-interval frames from the conditioning frames. Its performance bounds what any generative model could extract from that conditioning set; if the ceiling shows phase is unpredictable, the identifiability claim is empirically demonstrated rather than asserted.\n- **C9 integrator/thermostat-artifact control:** reference PSD from (a) NVE, (b) Langevin at two frictions, (c) two Δt_full values. Guards against crediting a thermostat/chain resonance as \"recovered dynamics\".\n- **C10 transfer control:** train on system A, evaluate on system B (expected: fast-band structure does **not** transfer; a transfer result would indicate a generic oscillator prior rather than learned dynamics — a genuinely informative surprise).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Metrics and concrete estimators\n\nAll angles **unwrapped**; cosine/sine parameterization used only for marginal/structural checks, never for spectral analysis.\n\n**(a) Fast-band normalized spectral shape (primary input).** Welch PSD: Hann window, 50% overlap, block length `T_block ≥ 1/(c·γ̃)` where `γ̃` is the mode FWHM in cm⁻¹ (resolution rule; `c` in cm/s), i.e. block length set from the measured linewidth in Step 0; PSDs normalized **within band** `B`: `Ŝ(f) = S(f)/∫_B S`. Shape discrepancy is the total-variation distance `D_shape(a,b) = ½∫_B |Ŝ_a − Ŝ_b| df ∈ [0,1]` — deliberately insensitive to total power (power is checked separately, so C1 cannot cheat).\n\n**(b) Reference noise floor.** `D_ref` = `D_shape` between two disjoint halves of the held-out reference of matched length. Any claim of \"perfect recovery\" beyond `D_ref` is not assessable and is capped.\n\n**(c) Pre-registered recovery ratio (decision statistic).** With `S_null ≡ D_shape` for **C1** and `S_ref ≡ D_ref`:\n\n> **ρ_shape = (S_null − S_model) / (S_null − S_ref)**, clipped to [−1, 2] and reported with a 95% block-bootstrap CI.\n\nInterpretation: ρ = 1 ⇒ reference-matching; ρ = 0 ⇒ no better than matched-power noise upsampling; ρ < 0 ⇒ worse than the null (a real and reportable outcome). **Non-informative branch (pre-declared):** if `S_null − S_ref < 0.15`, the contrast has no dynamic range, ρ is undefined for decision purposes, and the experiment is reported as **non-informative → re-scope the band or system**; this cannot be discovered after the fact because `S_null` and `S_ref` are computed and published in Step 0 / calibration.\n\n**(d) Secondary endpoints.**\n- `ρ_ACF`: same ratio built on the time-domain discrepancy `∫_0^{t_cut}|C_a − C_b| dt` with `t_cut` = one training interval τ (the horizon the model actually claims to fill).\n- Peak estimators per declared mode: peak frequency error `|f̂_gen − f₀|`; **alias-vs-truth screen** (`f̂_gen` closer to `f_alias` or `f₀`); linewidth ratio (dephasing rate); band-power ratio (equipartition/amplitude check, reported separately so it can never enter the primary endpoint).\n- **Ensemble conditional-mean response (\"kick response\"):** condition on M_rollout rollouts from each of `N_cond` pre-declared conditioning frames selected in the high-amplitude tail of the within-basin distribution; compute the ensemble mean trajectory and fit `e^{−t/T₂'}[A cos(2πf̂t) + B sin(2πf̂t)]`; report ringing amplitude ratio, `f̂`, and `T₂'`. This is the most interpretable evidence that a *conditional* fast oscillation is generated rather than averaged away.\n- Time-domain oscillation fidelity: band-pass-filtered envelope statistics, zero-crossing-interval distribution, and continuous wavelet time–frequency energy in B (segmentation-free cross-check for §5).\n- Per-mode amplitude via least-squares multi-sinusoid (or SSA/EMD as a robustness twin) fit over within-basin segments.\n- **Phase:** reported **only** as circular statistics — `R = |⟨exp(i(φ_gen − φ_ref))⟩|` over matched ensemble members, with Rayleigh test — and interpreted **only where §10 says phase is identifiable**. In the supercritical regime low `R` is recorded as the *expected identifiability outcome*, never as model failure, and never as success.\n\n**(e) Reference-quality metrics computed on every dataset** (segment counts, effective sample size, PSD CI) so that estimator noise is a reported quantity, not a hidden one.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Pre-registered decision rule (proposals for you and me to freeze before any run)\n\nPrimary endpoint: **median of `ρ_shape` over the pre-declared mode set `M`**, evaluated at the **strongly-supercritical** condition (`u ≥ 4`), reference-vs-C1 normalization.\n\n| Outcome | Proposal (pre-registration, to be fixed jointly) | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Recovery (yes)** | point estimate `ρ̂ ≥ 0.50` **and** lower 95% CI ≥ 0.25, **and** A-spec ablation degrades fast band selectively while vetoes hold, **and** C3 passes, **and** all §11 vetoes pass | \"at least half of the reference-vs-noise-upsampling distance recovered\"; the CI floor 0.25 blocks seed-lottery positives; requiring specificity + anti-hallucination prevents the noise-injection false positive |\n| **No recovery** | `ρ̂ ≤ 0.25` **or** upper 95% CI < 0.50 | a model recovering under a quarter of the distance is operationally indistinguishable from spline+noise |\n| **Indeterminate** | anything in between, **or** C2 fails, **or** §5 variance ratio ≥ 50%, **or** A-spec is globally degrading | each of these names the specific reason no claim is licensed |\n| **Non-informative** | `S_null − S_ref < 0.15` | no dynamic range in the contrast |\n\nMultiplicity: Holm correction across the modes in `M`; the median (not the best mode) is the endpoint, so mode cherry-picking after the fact is impossible. The threshold numbers above are **proposals, marked as such**, and are the only post-hoc-tunable knobs in the design — hence the requirement to freeze them with you before any run.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Identifiability: when is trajectory-level phase recoverable at all?\n\nTwo distinct mechanisms destroy information, and the design treats them separately.\n\n**(i) Aliasing (deterministic).** At training interval τ, a mode at `f₀` appears at `f_alias = |f₀ − round(f₀τ)/τ|`. For `τ > 2/f₀` (`u > 1/2`) the frequency itself is not identifiable from the data without further assumptions; for `f₀τ` near an integer the mode is indistinguishable from a static offset (§6 table: at `u = 1` the 100 cm⁻¹ **[ILLUS]** mode folds to ~0.1 cm⁻¹). The information about `f₀` is not degraded, it is **absent**.\n\n**(ii) Dynamical dephasing (stochastic).** For an underdamped torsion in a basin, `E[θ(t)|θ₀] = θ₀e^{−γt/2}[cos ω̃t + (γ/2ω̃)sin ω̃t]`, `ω̃=√(ω₀²−γ²/4)`. The predictable (phase-carrying) fraction is `≈ e^{−γt/2}`; with `T₂ = 2/γ`, the predictable horizon is `t_pred(ε) = T₂·ln(1/ε)`, and — importantly — **depends on γ, not on the mode frequency**. Illustrative (closed-form, §17): `T₂ = 0.5 ps → t_pred = 0.35 ps (ε=0.5), 0.80 ps (ε=0.2)`; `T₂ = 2 ps → 1.39 ps, 3.22 ps`. Practical MDGEN-style training intervals (ps–100 ps) exceed this by one to two orders of magnitude.\n\n**Consequence stated as the design's finding, not as a workaround.** There are therefore **three regimes of the question**, and the design measures the crossovers rather than choosing a side:\n- `u ≤ 0.25` and `τ ≲ t_pred`: phase is recoverable *in principle*; conditional-mean phase coherence `R` is a legitimate test and MDGEN is fairly judged by it.\n- `u ≳ 1/2`, `τ ≲ t_pred`: frequency is aliased but phase partially predictable — the interesting, under-explored middle where alias-vs-truth screening (§6) discriminates models.\n- `u ≳ 4` **and** `τ ≫ t_pred`: fast-mode phase is information-theoretically lost. **A phase-fidelity reading of \"accurately recover\" answers NO by construction here — this is a property of the data, not a model failure.** The only meaningful reading is statistical recovery of the fast band (frequencies, bandwidth, equipartitioned amplitude, ACF decay), which is what the primary endpoint measures. C8 (supervised ceiling) and C5 (AR fitted to subsampled data, peaking at `f_alias`) provide the empirical demonstration of this claim rather than leaving it as an assertion.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Slow structural checks as **veto conditions**, not success metrics\n\nRMSD distribution, Rg, RDF/SDF (solvent), φ/ψ free-energy surfaces and their KL distance, H-bond occupancy, and metastable transition rates (coarse MSM timescale agreement) are computed for every generative arm. Their **only** role is to invalidate a positive fast-band result, on grounds of \"it achieved fast structure by corrupting the distribution\".\nVeto rule (proposal): a fast-band \"yes\" is **void** if any of — marginal torsion distribution KL exceeds the full-rate positive control's value by more than its own CI; RMSD distribution shifts beyond the reference-vs-reference half-split bound; any MSM timescale deviates by more than a pre-declared relative tolerance; or RDF first-shell position/height moves outside the reference CI. A positive fast-band result with a veto failure is reported as **\"fast structure at the cost of equilibrium structure — does not answer the question.\"** A *negative* fast-band result never needs the vetoes.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Coordinates-only vs velocities-available metric sets\n\n| Diagnostic | velocities available | coordinates only (what is lost) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mode temperature / equipartition | direct, `⟨p²⟩ = kT` | **lost**; only configurational amplitude remains |\n| Short-time ballistic ACF (curvature at 0) | clean | degraded; requires `Δt_full ≤ T/10` |\n| RMS (second-moment) frequency `⟨ω²⟩ = −C̈(0)/C(0)` | exact and robust | available but only if `Δt_full ≲ T/10`; else unusable → **frequency identification becomes PSD-peak-only** |\n| Instantaneous frequency, wavelet ridge | both coordinates and momenta; mode-resolved | coordinate-only ridge, more sensitive to mode mixing |\n| Dephasing `T₂` | both routes (velocity ACF and coordinate ACF) agree → internal consistency check | single route; **internal consistency check is lost** |\n| Kick-response ensemble mean | can be initialized by velocity-kick (impulsive excitation of a chosen mode) | position-only displacement initialization; **selective mode excitation is lost**, so the test becomes less targeted |\n| Thermostat-artifact control (C9) | strong (kinetic side visible) | weak; NVE energy conservation unverifiable |\n\nHandling: the primary endpoint (`ρ_shape`) is deliberately chosen to be **computable coordinates-only**, so the headline answer does not silently depend on frame-file contents; everything lost is listed as a downgrade of the *secondary* evidence, and any Arm-M/branch-B dataset lacking velocities is analyzed under the coordinates-only protocol throughout.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Statistical plan\n\n- **Units of replication:** seeds (≥3; ≥5 if the codebase is non-deterministic), independent trajectory segments (within-basin, length-matched), and modes in `M`. Hierarchical variance components (seed / segment / mode) reported; CI from a **block bootstrap over segments** (blocks = segments, never frames), which respects serial correlation.\n- **Matched-budget rule:** all arms share parameter count, optimization steps, conditioning-frame count, and seed list; deviations are logged as protocol violations, not adjusted post hoc.\n- **Frozen artifacts (before any generative arm is scored):** mode set `M`, band `B`, `Δt_full`, `S_ref`, `S_null`, segmentation parameters and their sensitivity grid, the C1 noise-power matching procedure, the decision thresholds. Any change after unblinding is reported as an amendment.\n- **Reporting:** every figure shows reference, C1 (null), C2 (positive), C3 (suppressed), and each model arm; the alias frequency `f_alias(k)` is drawn on every PSD panel so a reader can inspect alias-fitting directly.\n- **Explicit anti-p-hacking statement:** the median-across-modes primary endpoint, the Holm correction, the pre-declared non-informative branch, and the veto architecture are what prevent the recovery ratio from being tuned after the fact.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Tiered feasibility plan (no budget assumed; run counts are the cost)\n\nRun count = (models) × (regimes) × (seeds); analytic/interpolation controls cost no training.\n\n**Tier 1 — MINIMAL (recommended default).** Arm T only (anharmonic rotor, semi-analytic ground truth). Regimes: resolved, near-Nyquist, sub-Nyquist, near-resonant `u≈1`, strongly-supercritical = 5. Arms: main model, C2 full-rate positive, C3 suppressed-reference model, A-spec ablation (i), A-spec ablation (ii). Seeds: 3.\n→ **5 × 5 × 3 = 75 model configurations**; all non-trained controls (C1, C4, C5, C6, C8-lite, segmentation-artifact null) free; C10 skipped.\n*Can support:* a clean answer to the primary question **for the assumed interface on a known-spectrum system**, the full identifiability crossover against theory, metric calibration (`S_ref`, `S_null`), and the whole veto/control logic. *Cannot support:* any biomolecular claim; any claim about a specific published checkpoint.\n*Why default:* it is the only tier that survives the no-data/no-GPU branch C, it is the tier where the identifiability claim is falsifiable against closed form, and it establishes that the metrics work (C2 passes) before any expensive arm is trusted.\n\n**Tier 2 — STANDARD.** Tier 1 + Arm M (one peptide), 4 regimes, main + C2 + 2 ablations, 3 seeds → `4 × 4 × 3 = 48` additional configurations; add C7 rollout disentanglement, C9 integrator/thermostat control, full §5 sensitivity grid, all slow vetoes.\n*Can additionally support:* that the Tier-1 conclusion transfers to real coupled anharmonic torsions, plus the structural-veto assessment. *Cannot:* claim cross-system generality.\n\n**Tier 3 — FULL.** Tier 2 + second molecular system, 6 regimes, N-ladder, C10 transfer, oracle conditioning, branch-B published-data replication, MSM kinetics, phase-coherence ladder across regimes → roughly 150–200 additional configurations.\n*Can additionally support:* cross-system generality, the phase-coherence-vs-`t_pred` ladder as a measured crossover, and external replication. *Cost:* this is where the design stops being cheap; nothing in the project obligates it.\n\nRecommendation: **commit Tier 1**; treat Tier 2 as the first escalation *only if* Tier 1 produces (a) C2 passing and (b) C1/C3 behaving as predicted, i.e. only if the instruments are demonstrably working.\n\n**Prerequisite for any tier (not available here):** a provisioned environment with numpy/scipy/torch plus an analysis stack (MDAnalysis or mdtraj) and, for Arm M, an MD engine; this workspace has none of those and no network, which is precisely why Tier 1 exists and why the design is written to be executable in an environment it does not describe.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Interpretation matrix (so a reviewer sees which answers are possible)\n\n| Primary `ρ_shape` | C2 positive ctrl | C3 anti-hallucination | A-spec pattern | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ≥0.5, CI floor ≥0.25 | pass | pass | selective fast-band loss | **Yes** — fast dynamics are generated, and by the named component |\n| ≥0.5 | pass | **fail** | any | **No** — recovered \"fast dynamics\" are injected noise |\n| ≥0.5 | pass | pass | global degradation | **Partially: yes for the endpoint, unresolved for the mechanism** |\n| ≥0.5 | pass | pass | selective, but a veto fails | **Void** — fast structure bought by corrupting equilibrium structure |\n| ≤0.25 | pass | pass | any | **No** — model is indistinguishable from spline-plus-noise in the fast band |\n| any | **fail** | any | any | **Indeterminate** — pipeline-limited; report and fix metrics |\n| any | any | any | any, with `S_null − S_ref < 0.15` | **Non-informative** — re-scope band/system |\n\n---\n\n## 16. Limitations\n\n1. **Interface assumption-dependence.** Every biomolecular conclusion is conditional on §2's contract. The two most dangerous deviations are (a) no per-conditioning-frame stochastic rollouts (kills the kick-response estimator) and (b) fixed N (collapses the specificity test to one ablation). Both are flagged inline with their consequences rather than assumed away.\n2. **Statistical, not trajectory-level.** By framing, a positive result licenses \"the model *generates* fast-band-consistent dynamics from sparse data\", never \"the model predicts the true trajectory\". §10 makes the boundary explicit and testable.\n3. **Answer-key asymmetry.** Only Arm T has analytic ground truth; Arm M conclusions are always partly contingent on the reference trajectory being an adequate representation of \"truth\" (force-field error is indistinguishable from model error in the fast band — a limitation the design can only flag, not remove).\n4. **Estimator vs model confounding.** PSD resolution, windowing leakage and finite segment counts can mimic or mask a fast-band deficit; mitigated (not eliminated) by C2, C6 surrogates, the §5 sensitivity grid, and reporting estimator CIs alongside model CIs.\n5. **Segmentation dependence** is the most likely silent failure mode; the 3×3 sensitivity grid + MSM-definition swap + segmentation-artifact null bound it, and the pre-declared ≥50% variance rule turns an unbounded worry into a stop condition.\n6. **Alias-fitting can masquerade as recovery.** Addressed by the alias-fingerprint screen and the near-resonant `u≈1` diagnostic, but a model could produce a partially correct spectrum for the wrong reason; the A-spec ablation is the remaining line of defense and it can be non-informative (§7.4 falsifier).\n7. **No results exist.** Nothing here has been executed; all thresholds are proposals and all numeric values labeled [ILLUS] come from closed-form period/frequency arithmetic, not measurement.\n\n---\n\n## 17. What I actually verified in this environment (closed-form only)\n\nPure-Python arithmetic, no scientific stack, no MD, no training:\n- Period–frequency conversions: 20 / 50 / 100 / 200 cm⁻¹ ↔ 1667.8 / 667.1 / 333.6 / 166.8 fs; hence Δt_full rules `≤ T/2` (Nyquist) and `≤ T/10` (ACF-curvature stability) — e.g. Δt_full ≤ 16.7 fs for a 200 cm⁻¹ mode.\n- The `u = τ/T_ref` k-sweep table in §6, including `f_Nyq = 1/(2τ)` and the exact folded (aliased) apparent frequencies at each `k`, and the **integer-u → DC** degeneracy that the main sweep must avoid.\n- The Langevin conditional-mean predictability horizon `t_pred = T₂·ln(1/ε)`, `T₂ = 2/γ` (frequency-independent): 0.35 / 0.80 ps for T₂ = 0.5 ps; 1.39 / 3.22 ps for T₂ = 2 ps.\n- Environment facts re-confirmed at the end of design work: `/workspace` still contains only `task.json`; numpy remains absent (no installs attempted)."}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-7de7d8c7-70cd-4ce6-8c8c-c134dccefb1b", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": null, "kind": "ActionEvent"}
